\details{
For \code{student()}, the degrees of freedom parameter is estimated by default (\code{df = NULL}).
You can fix it at a specific value by providing a number > 1 (e.g., \code{df = 3}).

The \code{gengamma()} family was implemented by J.T. Thorson and uses the Prentice
(1974) parameterization such that the lognormal occurs as the internal
parameter \code{gengamma_Q} (reported in \code{print()} or \code{summary()} as
"Generalized gamma Q") approaches 0. If Q matches \code{phi} the distribution
should be the gamma.

The \code{ordbeta()} family implements the ordered-beta regression of Kubinec
(2023) for continuous responses on the closed unit interval \verb{[0, 1]} with
point masses at the endpoints. It is a parsimonious alternative to
zero-one-inflated beta models because the three components (zeros, ones,
and continuous (0, 1) values) share a single linear predictor. Two
internal cutpoints are estimated and reported on the response scale
(\code{plogis()}).

The \code{nbinom2} negative binomial parameterization is the NB2 where the
variance grows quadratically with the mean (Hilbe 2011).

The \code{nbinom1} negative binomial parameterization lets the variance grow
linearly with the mean (Hilbe 2011).

The \verb{censored_*()} families treat some counts as known only to lie within a
range, e.g., to account for hook competition in longline surveys (Watson et
al. 2023). Supply the bounds with \code{sdmTMB(censored_upper = ...)}.
\itemize{
\item \code{censored_poisson()}, \code{censored_nbinom1()}, and \code{censored_nbinom2()} have
no upper limit on the count; use an offset of log hooks for effort.
\item \code{censored_binomial()} and \code{censored_betabinomial()} take the number of
trials (e.g., hooks) via \code{weights}, which caps the count.
}

All but \code{censored_poisson()} need the RTMB backend (the default). See
\code{censored_method} in \code{\link[=sdmTMBcontrol]{sdmTMBcontrol()}} for how \code{censored_betabinomial()}
computes its likelihood, and the
\href{https://sdmTMB.github.io/sdmTMB/articles/hook-competition.html}{hook competition article}.

The families ending in \verb{_mix()} are 2-component mixtures where each
distribution has its own mean but they share a scale parameter.
(Thorson et al. 2011). See the model-description vignette for details.
The parameter \code{p_extreme = plogis(logit_p_extreme)} is the probability of the extreme (larger)
mean and \code{exp(log_ratio_mix) + 1} is the ratio of the larger extreme
mean to the "regular" mean. You can see these parameters in
\code{model$sd_report}. The parameter \code{p_extreme} can be fixed a priori and passed
in as a proportion for these families.

The default first-component link (\code{link1}) for delta models of
\code{type = "standard"} is \code{"logit"}. For \code{type = "poisson-link"}, the
default \code{link1} is \code{"log"}.

The \code{compois} family is the Conway-Maxwell-Poisson distribution
parameterized by its mean (Huang 2017), as in \pkg{glmmTMB}. It can model
underdispersed (\code{phi < 1}) as well as overdispersed (\code{phi > 1}) counts, where
\code{phi} = \code{1/nu} and the variance is approximately \code{mu * phi}. It needs the
RTMB backend (the default).

The \code{stdcurve} family is for environmental DNA (eDNA) qPCR data, which come
with a second, laboratory data set of standards. The response is the qPCR
cycle threshold (Ct), with \code{0} meaning no amplification. The linear
predictor \eqn{\eta} is log DNA concentration (a log link). Each PCR plate
\eqn{j} has its own standard curve: a logistic detection model,
\eqn{\mathrm{logit}(p) = \xi_{3,j} + \xi_{4,j} f(\eta)}, and a Gaussian model
of positive Ct values with mean \eqn{\xi_{1,j} + \xi_{2,j} \eta} and
standard deviation \code{phi}, where \eqn{f} is \code{exp()}
(\code{detection = "concentration"}) or the identity
(\code{detection = "log_concentration"}). The plate coefficients are independent
normal random effects (no correlation among a plate's coefficients) with
estimated means and standard deviations. They are informed by the
\code{standards}, for which \eqn{\eta} is the known log concentration, and by
the field samples. See \code{\link[=get_stdcurve]{get_stdcurve()}} for the estimates.

The among-plate standard deviations (\code{ln_std_sigma}; in the order Ct
intercept, Ct slope, detection intercept, detection slope) need standards
from several plates to be estimated. With few plates, the detection
standard deviations can collapse toward zero, which \code{\link[=sanity]{sanity()}} flags. One
can then be fixed at a small value with
\code{sdmTMBcontrol(map = list(ln_std_sigma = factor(c(1, 2, NA, 3))))} and a
matching \code{start} value.

Predictions with \code{\link[=predict.sdmTMB]{predict.sdmTMB()}} are of log concentration
(\code{type = "link"}) or concentration (\code{type = "response"}), and do not depend
on plates; derived quantities such as \code{\link[=get_index]{get_index()}} are of concentration.
\code{fitted()} and response residuals are expected Ct values (detection
probability times the mean positive Ct) on each sample's plate. Deviance
and Pearson residuals are not available. Needs the RTMB backend (the
default).
}
